INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Fluorescent hum. Dryers turning.

CORA BELLAMY (40s, scrubs, tired) folds towels. Precisely. Corners aligned.

Her sister, WANDA BELLAMY (40s, leather jacket), sits on a washer, swinging a boot.

WANDA
Nice night.

CORA
It's one a.m.

WANDA
Still nice.

Cora folds. Wanda watches.

WANDA (CONT'D)
Mom called.

CORA
Did she.

WANDA
Said you hung up on her.

CORA
She said I hung up on her.

WANDA
She said she was sorry, too.

CORA
She said that?

WANDA
Not in words.

Cora sets a towel down. Squares it.

CORA
Why are you here, Wanda?

WANDA
She asked me to tell you something.

CORA
Then tell me.

WANDA
She sold the house. She sold it last week. She's moving to Tucson.

The dryer buzzes. Neither of them moves.

CORA
Without telling me.

WANDA
Without telling either of us. I found out at the bank.

Cora picks up the next towel. Her hands shake. She folds it anyway.
